import DocsLayout from "../../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Proton">
    <h1>Proton</h1>

    <p class="docs-lead">
        Proton is Valve's compatibility layer for running many Windows games
        through Steam on Linux.
    </p>

    <h2>How Proton works</h2>

    <p>
        Proton combines technologies including Wine and DXVK with additional
        compatibility work from Valve and the Linux gaming community.
    </p>

    <p>
        Steam downloads and manages Proton versions automatically.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-note">
        <strong>Octlitch does not preinstall Proton</strong>

        <p>
            Steam manages Proton itself. This avoids duplicating compatibility
            tools in the base operating system.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Enable compatibility for Windows games</h2>

    <p>
        Open Steam settings and find the compatibility or Steam Play section.
    </p>

    <p>
        Steam can use Proton automatically for supported titles. You can also
        enable compatibility for additional Windows games when required.
    </p>

    <h2>Choose a Proton version for a game</h2>

    <p>
        To select a specific Proton version for one game:
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>Open your Steam library.</li>
        <li>Right-click the game.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>Properties</strong>.</li>
        <li>Open the <strong>Compatibility</strong> section.</li>
        <li>
            Enable the option to force a specific Steam Play compatibility
            tool.
        </li>
        <li>Select the Proton version you want to test.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Proton Experimental</h2>

    <p>
        Proton Experimental contains newer compatibility fixes than the
        regular stable Proton releases.
    </p>

    <p>
        It can be useful for recently released games or titles that require
        newer fixes.
    </p>

    <p>
        If a game works correctly with the default Proton version, there is
        usually no reason to force Proton Experimental.
    </p>

    <h2>Native Linux games</h2>

    <p>
        Some Steam games provide a native Linux build.
    </p>

    <p>
        Steam may use the native version by default instead of Proton.
        Forcing Proton is optional and may sometimes help if the Windows build
        works better than the native version.
    </p>

    <h2>Changing Proton versions</h2>

    <p>
        Different games may behave differently with different Proton releases.
    </p>

    <p>
        If a game stops working after changing versions, return to its
        Compatibility settings and try the previous version or the default
        selected by Steam.
    </p>

    <h2>Game compatibility</h2>

    <p>
        Not every Windows game works perfectly under Proton.
    </p>

    <p>
        Compatibility can depend on:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>the game engine</li>
        <li>graphics APIs</li>
        <li>anti-cheat support</li>
        <li>launchers and account systems</li>
        <li>the selected Proton version</li>
        <li>graphics drivers</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Anti-cheat</h2>

    <p>
        Some multiplayer games use anti-cheat systems that may not support
        Linux or Proton.
    </p>

    <p>
        In those cases the game may fail to start or multiplayer functionality
        may be unavailable even when the rest of the game would otherwise run.
    </p>

    <h2>Basic troubleshooting</h2>

    <p>
        If a game does not start, first try:
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>Restart Steam.</li>
        <li>Check for Octlitch system updates.</li>
        <li>Update Steam and Flatpak applications.</li>
        <li>Verify the game's files in Steam.</li>
        <li>Try another Proton version.</li>
        <li>Check that your graphics driver is working correctly.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        Update the system:
    </p>

    <pre><code>sudo dnf upgrade --refresh</code></pre>

    <p>
        Update Flatpak applications:
    </p>

    <pre><code>flatpak update</code></pre>

    <h2>Proton logs</h2>

    <p>
        Advanced troubleshooting can use Proton's logging facility.
    </p>

    <p>
        Add the following to the game's Steam launch options:
    </p>

    <pre><code>PROTON_LOG=1 %command%</code></pre>

    <p>
        Launch the game once. Proton will generate a log file in your home
        directory using the game's Steam application ID.
    </p>

    <p>
        Remove the launch option when you no longer need diagnostic logging.
    </p>

    <h2>Custom compatibility tools</h2>

    <p>
        Third-party Proton builds exist, but they are not part of the
        standard Octlitch installation.
    </p>

    <p>
        Start with the Proton versions supplied by Steam before adding custom
        compatibility tools.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-next">
        <a href="/documentation/troubleshooting/">
            Next: Common problems →
        </a>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
